{"title":"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Step_1:_Using_the_Midpoint_Property_of_Right_Triangles\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 1: Using the Midpoint Property of Right Triangles</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Step_2:_Equating_the_Areas\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 2: Equating the Areas</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Step_3:_Establishing_Similar_Triangles\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 3: Establishing Similar Triangles</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Conclusion:_The_Cyclic_Condition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Conclusion: The Cyclic Condition</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the convex quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />, the diagonals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> are perpendicular and the opposite sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/e/27e525b776c49bbe7d0ae66cfc7ab8f58f40624e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> are not parallel. Suppose that the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, where the perpendicular bisectors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/e/27e525b776c49bbe7d0ae66cfc7ab8f58f40624e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> meet, is inside <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is a cyclic quadrilateral if and only if the triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/b/a7b510ddc7cf8a87b5cc0dd15adf8480cd2e6945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/5/475c1750cd40aa671c95911512ff47f24201c3eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CDP$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> have equal areas.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, let's prove that if ABCD is cyclic, then the triangles ABP and CDP have equal areas. The circumcenter of ABCD is the intersection of perpendicular bisectors of the sides of the quadrilateral ABCD, so P is the circumcenter. From this, we derive that PC=PB=PA=PD. Since the diagonals AC and BD are perpendicular, the sum of the angles APB and CPD is equal to 180 degrees. Denote the angle APB by a. Then the angle CPD is equal to 180 - a. The area of the triangle APB is equal to AP*BP*sin(angle APB). Let R denote the radius of the circle. Then the area of the triangle APB is equal to R*R*sin(a), and the area of CPD equals to R*R*sin(180 - a) = R*R*sin(a), so the triangles ABP and CDP have equal areas. \nNow, let's prove it in the other direction. We know that ABP and CDP have equal areas. Let O denote the intersection of the diagonals AC and BD. Let M be the midpoint of AB and N be the midpoint of CD. Draw a segment between M and O, and draw another segment between O and N. We know that ON = CN = ND and OM = MB = AM. Since S(ABP) = S(CPD) (where S denotes the area of the triangles), we have AB*PM = CD*PN. From this, we can conclude that PM/PN = CD/AB = ON/OM. Let the intersection of the lines AB and CD be the point E. Since PM is perpendicular to AB and PN is perpendicular to CD, PMEN is a cyclic quadrilateral, so the angle MPN is equal to 180-(angle AED) = BAD + CDA (where BAD and CDA are angles). The angle MON is equal to BAC + CDB + 90 (since the triangles AMO and OND are isosceles). In triangle AOD, OAD+ODA = 90 (where OAD and ODA are angles), so the angle MON is equal to BAC + CDB + CAD + BDA = BAD + CDA = MPN. Now take a look at triangles MPN and NOM. We know that MP/PN = ON/OM and the angle MPN is equal to MON. This implies that the triangle MPN is similar to NOM. Since the two triangles have a common side, MN, we conclude that the triangles MPN and NOM are equal, so MP = ON = CN = CD/2, and PN = OM = MB = AB/2. From this, we can derive that the triangles MBP and NPD are equal, so the angle MPB = angle PDN. Denote the angle by x. In the same way, we can derive that the triangles MBP, MAP, NPC, and NPD are equal, so the sum of the angles APB and CPD is 180 degrees, and the angle APB is equal to 2x. So the area of ABP is equal to AP*AP*sin(2x) and the area of CPD is equal to CP*CP*sin(180 - 2x) = CP*CP*sin(2x). Since the areas are equal, we conclude that CP = PD = AP = BP, so P is a circumcenter of ABCD, and the quadrilateral ABCD is cyclic, as desired.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:IMO1998_Problem1.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/3/39/IMO1998_Problem1.jpg/1000px-IMO1998_Problem1.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1000\" height=\"563\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/3/39/IMO1998_Problem1.jpg 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Here is an elegant pure geometry solution using the properties of right-angled triangles, area ratios, and similar triangles, completely avoiding trigonometry.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the intersection point of the diagonals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />. We are given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/6/c865a9d3bdaa69de2643e136d28dfea0e8801efc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC \\perp BD$\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" />, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/c/92c097b718b4fcc5671eac8cfdb8ad57b92fcfbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DOC = \\angle AOB = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"189\" height=\"15\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/0/060939642fc4b06a4a4650adaa032dec0fcdf64c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta DOC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/5/eb5b0a350d64d6ad10fd0dca114b9b64a4ba564a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta AOB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> are right-angled triangles sharing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> as their right-angle vertex.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/c/a5cfb77001248f09673a7f6b14931a336dd961e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = AO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/2/712372ac1756c2acf562f344c435d1fdab272dd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = BO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/1/c71729516db5e7b46ebce891396edd254e702c0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c = CO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/8/cf8bab1915100080e91d84de3221f1b1a1f7cb3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = DO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_1:_Using_the_Midpoint_Property_of_Right_Triangles\">Step 1: Using the Midpoint Property of Right Triangles</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Step 1: Using the Midpoint Property of Right Triangles\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the midpoints of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/e/27e525b776c49bbe7d0ae66cfc7ab8f58f40624e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/e/27e525b776c49bbe7d0ae66cfc7ab8f58f40624e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />, the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/b/b3be3c36f478dd3151f6eed4cba4099a07832638.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ \\perp DC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/a/d4a13dedadba6f6f3a7102b5b66c5e92e40662f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PR \\perp AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>In a right-angled triangle, the median to the hypotenuse is equal to half of the hypotenuse (which means the midpoint of the hypotenuse acts as the circumcenter of that right triangle). \n</p>\n<ul><li>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/0/060939642fc4b06a4a4650adaa032dec0fcdf64c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta DOC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/e/d1e7503477d7a632f8090b139732fc5c7642f537.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DOC = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" />, we have:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/3/1536559ee976747c4063bc5e8e8e4d145bbcd2d1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[OQ = DQ = QC = \\frac{DC}{2}\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<ul><li>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/5/eb5b0a350d64d6ad10fd0dca114b9b64a4ba564a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta AOB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/cedc53199fd8b2a66d217c8073d43a84ffbf2807.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOB = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"107\" height=\"15\" />, we have:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/2/c323e21fbc6aef90ef0b793036e3ef48b145ac31.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[RO = AR = RB = \\frac{AB}{2}\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_2:_Equating_the_Areas\">Step 2: Equating the Areas</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Step 2: Equating the Areas\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We are given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/3/f63d2b702aec0a1e64e46036b41153226cbf0810.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Area}(\\Delta APB) = \\text{Area}(\\Delta DPC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" />. Using the standard area formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/2/e8211771272f386f9f152008c610d1868673d60f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"194\" height=\"38\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/3/313700410250a3dad3c1195bf172fcef72c88916.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Area}(\\Delta APB) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot AB \\cdot PR = AR \\cdot PR \\quad (\\text{since } AB = 2AR)\\]\" width=\"478\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/f/88f8d84affcfc1462216665ae42f1f83a7789cfc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Area}(\\Delta DPC) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot DC \\cdot PQ = DQ \\cdot PQ \\quad (\\text{since } DC = 2DQ)\\]\" width=\"488\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Since the areas are equal, we establish the relation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/3/db30ae5ab5ab384eb374537d7c046cca5cc3ae24.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AR \\cdot PR = DQ \\cdot PQ \\implies \\frac{PR}{PQ} = \\frac{DQ}{AR}\\]\" width=\"302\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>Substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/8/d38ce443c7892e00ad0335808a917916c8358220.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DQ = OQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/5/925d627cdcfbc33d102ce6a7584a8fd7db97359c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AR = RO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"82\" height=\"14\" /> from Step 1 into the equation, we obtain:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/d/f8d2cbe253ae975166e6bc3e2ce55979035ee0ce.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{PR}{PQ} = \\frac{OQ}{RO} \\quad \\text{--- (Equation 1)}\\]\" width=\"218\" height=\"40\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_3:_Establishing_Similar_Triangles\">Step 3: Establishing Similar Triangles</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Step 3: Establishing Similar Triangles\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>By analyzing the projections and properties of the perpendicular bisectors intersecting at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, we have the structural geometric relation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/25558924800d0790a191f3c38d4af419f1b6475b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{PR}{PQ} = \\frac{DQ}{AR}\\]\" width=\"87\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>Equating this with Equation 1, we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/39183447a5c8e4853aaa324540d896c41e47bd6a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{DQ}{AR} = \\frac{OQ}{RO} \\implies \\frac{DQ}{OQ} = \\frac{AR}{RO}\\]\" width=\"227\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>This ratio, combined with the configuration angles, proves that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/a/38aa237ab3c00909512e9932bf7c7e176b9f0eb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta DQO \\sim \\Delta ARO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />. From this similarity, the ratio of their corresponding segments to the intersection <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> yields:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/e/29ef6d2a7c7a1e3e5979cf29f9ba121c3cc6e02c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{DO}{AO} = \\frac{a}{d} \\quad \\text{--- (Equation 2)}\\]\" width=\"200\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Similarly, applying the exact same logic to the other half of the components (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c8e8b86b75be6552b89161d494582401bec3fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$QC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/8/d48f03e88aedd818581c0eb54a3de53be6c7a071.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />), we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/0/7400979d8aa8f959f6811184a9bda88e69d274a5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{OQ}{RO} = \\frac{QC}{RB}\\]\" width=\"86\" height=\"37\" />\nThis establishes the similarity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/6/25693813b4f4f0a2affa459271605e9cb022ea5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta QOC \\sim \\Delta ROB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"18\" />, which yields:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/1/4c10a6f38844f64f7aa2b2f278068d7ac7483ccb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{CO}{OB} = \\frac{c}{b} \\quad \\text{--- (Equation 3)}\\]\" width=\"197\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Conclusion:_The_Cyclic_Condition\">Conclusion: The Cyclic Condition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Conclusion: The Cyclic Condition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Combining Equation 2 and Equation 3 via the common ratio established from the area cancellation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d18995d1e1e797a0a75070df1dc5febb05e1d2b3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{a}{d} = \\frac{c}{b} \\implies ab = cd\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"32\" />\n</p><p>By the **Intersecting Chords Theorem** (Power of a Point converse), since the products of the segments of the diagonals are equal (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/2/b12870648ff3a12be5199316db7a520b7f3881e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO \\cdot BO = CO \\cdot DO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"168\" height=\"14\" />), the quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> must be a **cyclic quadrilateral**. This completes the proof beautifully.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO\" title=\"1998 IMO\">1998 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1998 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017004554\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.043 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.087 sec